\paragraph{Algorithmic and Stiffness Takeaways.}
The computational performance metrics in Table~\ref{tab:executive_campaign_matrix} highlight how phase-space geometry governs stiff numerical integration:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Radiative Quenching and Stiffness (FLOSS):} In low-roughness, snow-insulated environments (FLOSS), fast TKE quenching pushes the state vector rapidly toward the laminar manifold boundary. The steep gradients near collapse induce local stiff time scales ($\varepsilon \ll 1$), driving the Jacobian condition number to $\kappa(J) \sim 10^6$. The adaptive \texttt{Rodas5P} integrator automatically scales down step sizes ($\sim 890$ steps) to maintain $C^1$ trajectory accuracy without solver failure.
    \item \textbf{Continuous Shear Regularization (CASES-99):} In contrast, continuous mechanical shear over higher surface roughness (CASES-99) keeps the system in an active, well-mixed state. The absence of abrupt fast-manifold collapse keeps the system Jacobian well-conditioned ($\kappa(J) < 10^4$), requiring less than half the integrator steps ($\sim 420$ steps) to complete a $12$-hour nocturnal integration.
\end{itemize}